Each style has a review sheet generated from the candidate:

- the typography specimen, the every-block fixture, every chart type, the map, flow and pivot table, and the flagship, at 320, 390 and 1440 px and as PDF;
- the same content in the other four styles beside it, so structural differences show: outlines and shading, legend and title positions, Scientific's captions below charts in boxes;
- the style's declared structure (where legends and titles sit, outlines or none) verified against the geometry model, and STY-52's measured checks;
- entrance clips in that style;
- STY-51's three answers.

You review a sheet when a style is created and when its tokens or structure change. A review agent on the model you use at the time judges it first, including distinctiveness beyond the mechanical check, and its verdict sits on the sheet (Daniel's DEL-68 answer). A mechanical check flags two styles whose structural declarations are identical. Report entries that change any capture in a style show the sheet's diff. Once you accept a style, later changes to it come one by one through the before-and-after report (DEL-69): crops at 390 and 1440 px in every affected style, all five for a style-wide change, and frame strips and clips for motion.
